 means that you often need  to mix multiple kinds of domains for  instance an electric kettle model might  need combination of electrical and  thermal models along the same line you  need to be able to combine continuous  and discrete behavior yes as real-life  system would sister model is built from  the ground up to meet these demands so  let us look at them one by one beginning  with the last one let's get back in time  a bit and look at the good old-fashioned  trebuchet the actual throwing a flight  of the stone is best model as a  continuous system while the logic for  how the catapult is governed might be  better modeled as a discrete process  furthermore you would probably like to  model the bouncing as discrete events in  system or ease you can easily combine  these two as shown by the animation of a  very bouncy stoner I mentioned an  electric kettle as an example of a  system that requires components from  different domains so let's explore this  a bit further as you can see in the  model diagram we have pointed out four  different types of components  we have a resistor or thermistor that is  used to heat up the water so this  resistor is basically the coil in our  kettle and then we have the water here  we have a temperature sensor that takes  us from from thermal over to numerix and  that then numerix is used connected here  to a logic block or a girlfriend from  numerix to logical decision and this  logical operation or signal is fed back  to our opening switch where the logic is  used to control an electrical component  so we see this kind of loop of things  interconnecting different domains so  even if this is a very small system it  required a bunch of different domains to  be able to model it correctly as you saw  in the previous example the components  the system or a generally easy to easy  recognizable this is a key aspect if you  want to model a large system such as  this model of our other flaps in a  cessna conquest four-for-one in this  case the model developer has developed  his own components for the top level  model one component for the pilot one  for the electrical system one for the  flaps for the landing gear motor  hydraulic system and so on and you can  easily recognize them in in the top  level view you can also dig in to the  component by clicking on it so for  instance if we open the electrical  circuit we can see that is built up by  bulbs and wires sensors and so on and in  the same manner if we click into the  flaps we see that the flaps consists of  an hydraulic system and a mechanical  system that interact  and on top level all these systems  interact with each other of course  so this capability of having something  look and being organized as a real-life  system is crucial if you want to be able  to develop something large and  communicate that with others finally and  probably the most important aspect once  you have invested a time in developing a  model you would pro like to get as much  mileage as possible from it while  developing a model and simulating with  different parameters might be very  valuable in itself the ability of  creating virtual labs optimizing your  system performing sensitivity analysis  and so on is key to get that mileage  that is exactly what the combination  system order Mathematica gives you so in  this seminar we're focusing on on the  drag-and-drop and connect part that  enables all these and the kind of  one-click simulation but beware that  there's more to it in system model and  the Wolfram language it allows you to  full simulation control and the an  unlimited set of analysis and design  tools if you want to combine your model  your system model with machine learning  you can do that if you want to do  control design you can do that if you  want to document it you can do that and  so on so almost whatever you would like  to do is up to you how you want to  combine it and how you want to analyze  it and then of course you can share in  several different ways including through  the vault from language  so and that concludes my part of this  conference and I will soon hand over to  Patrick Eichenberg who will take you  through how you can start using system  or by exploring ready-made models how  you can use these models to modify them  and adjust them according to your needs  and finally in the last session of the  day how you develop your own models with  drag and drop all these sessions are a  bit more hands-on you will see the  actual tool in use and it will get it  give going in pace so you can follow  along if you have an installation on  your computer and as I said before  you're welcome to ask any questions to  reach out and we'll try to assist with  answers thank you very much
 

